(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-master-drill"]={"title":"Master Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is soothing on an inflamed eye, so it may be used even when bacterial infection is not confirmed?","choices":["Erythromycin","Azithromycin","Ciprofloxacin","Tobramycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is soothing on the inflamed eye, and it is dirt cheap. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which class of eye drop causes REBOUND HYPEREMIA if used for too long?","choices":["Olopatadine","Naphazoline","Cromolyn","Ketorolac"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Naphazoline is a topical vasoconstrictor, and prolonged use leads to rebound hyperemia after discontinuation; nasal decongestant sprays share the risk. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which class is FIRST LINE for glaucoma and the most commonly used?","choices":["Timolol","Dorzolamide","Latanoprost","Pilocarpine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The prostaglandin analogs, which increase aqueous outflow. Beta blockers are second line. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is the MAINSTAY of acute otitis media therapy, given in a higher dose to overcome Streptococcus pneumoniae resistance?","choices":["Azithromycin","Cefdinir","Ceftriaxone","Amoxicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin is the mainstay for acute otitis media; the higher dose is what overcomes Streptococcus pneumoniae resistance. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which drug causes a DRY COUGH from bradykinin accumulating in the lungs?","choices":["Lisinopril","Losartan","Amlodipine","Metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors stop bradykinin breakdown; the cough is not dose- or agent-related. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which statin has MINIMAL CYP metabolism, so CYP3A4 inhibitors affect it least?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin's major metabolic route is listed as minimal CYP, unlike the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which drug class is first-line antianginal therapy when there are no contraindications?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Beta blockers","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are the first-line antianginal therapy in the absence of contraindications. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is used for prophylaxis of ophthalmia neonatorum?","choices":["Gatifloxacin","Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin","Erythromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The 0.5% ointment, for superficial conjunctival or corneal infection and for newborn prophylaxis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient asks how long they may use a vasoconstrictor eye drop. What is the limit?","choices":["Less than two weeks","Less than 72 hours","Less than six weeks","No limit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasoconstrictor drops are for short-term use under two weeks; prolonged use leads to rebound hyperemia after they are stopped. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class changes EYELASH LENGTH and IRIS COLOR?","choices":["Brimonidine","Bimatoprost","Betaxolol","Brinzolamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The prostaglandin analogs, which is why one of them is also marketed for lash growth. They also cause conjunctival hyperemia. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which ORAL antibiotic is used for SEVERE or RESISTANT otitis media, or when the child had antibiotics in the previous month?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Piperacillin-tazobactam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin-clavulanate is the step up for severe or resistant disease or recent antibiotic exposure. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient cannot tolerate an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor's cough. Which drug is the SWITCH?","choices":["Enalapril","Captopril","Ramipril","Valsartan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not cause cough and have a lower incidence of angioedema, so patients may be switched to them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which statin is a CYP3A4 SUBSTRATE, raised by verapamil and grapefruit juice?","choices":["Lovastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which beta blocker belongs to the third generation of agents?","choices":["Metoprolol","Carvedilol","Propranolol","Atenolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol and labetalol are the third-generation beta blockers. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is considerably more expensive and given less frequently, so it is not used as often clinically?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Given less often than erythromycin, but the cost keeps it off the shelf. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A patient using an over-the-counter vasoconstrictor drop has had no improvement. At what point should they stop and see a provider?","choices":["Two weeks","24 hours","Seven days","72 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No improvement within 72 hours means something more serious may be going on. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why must a prostaglandin analog NOT be dosed more than once daily?","choices":["Exceeding once daily INHIBITS the pressure-lowering effect","It causes rebound hyperemia","It raises the risk of cataract","It causes bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. More is worse, not better, which is counterintuitive and therefore worth holding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which ORAL antibiotic is the STANDARD therapy for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis, because Haemophilus influenzae often makes beta-lactamase?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V potassium","Dicloxacillin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clavulanate protects the penicillin from beta-lactamase, which Haemophilus influenzae often produces in sinusitis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has an INTRAVENOUS active form?","choices":["Lisinopril","Benazepril","Enalapril","Quinapril"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Enalapril is converted to enalaprilat, which is the intravenous form. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which statin is metabolized mainly by CYP2C9?","choices":["Simvastatin","Atorvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Fluvastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluvastatin's major metabolic enzyme is CYP2C9, not CYP3A4. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which drug is non-cardioselective, so it is avoided in patients with asthma?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Atenolol","Verapamil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-cardioselective beta blockers such as propranolol are to be avoided in asthma. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic leaves a white precipitate in about one in six patients?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A white precipitate in roughly 17%, which is specific to it among the fluoroquinolones. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Topical vasoconstrictors act on which receptor LOCALLY in the eye?","choices":["Alpha 2","Beta 1","Alpha 1","Beta 2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Locally alpha 1, which constricts the conjunctival vessels. Systemically these imidazolines target alpha 2 instead, which is what makes an accidental ingestion dangerous. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is BETA 1 SELECTIVE?","choices":["Timolol","Carteolol","Levobunolol","Betaxolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The selective one, so it carries less risk of bronchoconstriction in a patient with asthma. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone is the single-drug alternative to clindamycin plus cefixime for sinusitis in a penicillin-allergic patient?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin","Azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin is the fluoroquinolone named as the alternative for penicillin-allergic sinusitis; it has good activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has a SHORT half-life, the exception to once-daily dosing?","choices":["Lisinopril","Captopril","Ramipril","Trandolapril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors are once daily; captopril's short half-life is the exception. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statin is cleared by GLUCURONIDATION (UGT enzymes) rather than CYP?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Pitavastatin","Fluvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pitavastatin's major metabolic enzymes are UGT1A3 and UGT2B7. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which antianginal carries the warning to avoid rapid discontinuation?","choices":["Amlodipine","Isosorbide mononitrate","Verapamil","Metoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients taking a beta blocker such as metoprolol are taught to avoid rapid discontinuation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class must be avoided in a patient with a sulfonamide allergy?","choices":["Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin","Tobramycin","Trimethoprim with polymyxin B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, so a sulfa allergy rules it out. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antihistamine is available over the counter?","choices":["Azelastine","Ketotifen","Bepotastine","Emedastine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The over-the-counter agent among the ophthalmic antihistamines. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why is a NONSELECTIVE beta blocker more efficacious in the eye but worse tolerated?","choices":["There are more beta 1 receptors in the eye","It penetrates the cornea better","There are more beta 2 receptors in the eye","It has a longer half-life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. So blocking both gives more effect, at the cost of beta 2 effects elsewhere. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin is escalated to by injection when oral therapy for otitis media fails or is not tolerated?","choices":["Cefdinir","Cephalexin","Cefixime","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ceftriaxone is given by injection, either intramuscular or intravenous, for failed therapy or when oral drugs are not tolerated. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which drug raises POTASSIUM, especially with renal disease or a potassium-sparing diuretic?","choices":["Candesartan","Nifedipine","Doxazosin","Clonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers (like ACE inhibitors) cause hyperkalemia, most often with renal disease or potassium-sparing diuretics. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which statin can be used as HIGH-INTENSITY therapy?","choices":["Pravastatin","Atorvastatin","Fluvastatin","Lovastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Only atorvastatin and rosuvastatin reach high intensity, lowering LDL (low-density lipoprotein) by 50% or more. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which drug can cause nightmares, sexual dysfunction and worsened claudication?","choices":["Amlodipine","Isosorbide dinitrate","Propranolol","Diltiazem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers such as propranolol cause fatigue, sexual dysfunction, nightmares and worsened claudication. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by preventing transfer of mucopeptides into the growing wall?","choices":["Erythromycin","Gentamicin","Ofloxacin","Bacitracin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A cell wall agent, which sets it apart from the protein-synthesis and DNA agents alongside it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"H1 receptor blockers are not really antagonists. What are they?","choices":["Inverse agonists","Non-competitive antagonists","Partial agonists","Irreversible blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They cause histamine receptor INACTIVATION, and they remain competitive with histamine. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class risks worsening heart failure, bradycardia, heart block and increased airway resistance?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic beta blockade from a drop, which is why the nasolacrimal duct is worth occluding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which third-generation cephalosporin is the oral choice for otitis media in a penicillin-allergic patient or after failed therapy?","choices":["Cephalexin","Ceftriaxone","Cefdinir","Cefazolin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cefdinir is the third-generation oral cephalosporin named for penicillin allergy and for failed therapy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker treats PAROXYSMAL SUPRAVENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA?","choices":["Amlodipine","Felodipine","Nisoldipine","Verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only the non-dihydropyridines slow atrioventricular conduction, so only they treat supraventricular tachycardia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is the OTHER statin, besides atorvastatin, that reaches HIGH INTENSITY?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Simvastatin","Pitavastatin","Pravastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are the only two high-intensity statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker slows atrioventricular conduction the most?","choices":["Nifedipine","Verapamil","Amlodipine","Felodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil shows the largest fall in atrioventricular conduction, along with lower heart rate and contractility. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class binds the 50S ribosome to block protein synthesis?","choices":["Tobramycin","Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The macrolide mechanism: it binds the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks protein synthesis, which stops bacterial growth. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which class works within MINUTES and is typically preferred for ocular allergy?","choices":["Cromolyn","Lodoxamide","Nedocromil","Olopatadine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antihistamines have an onset within minutes and are preferred over mast cell stabilizers for ocular allergy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class works by blocking beta receptors in the ciliary body epithelium to reduce aqueous production?","choices":["Levobunolol","Travoprost","Carbachol","Apraclonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Less catecholamine activation, less cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP), less aqueous made. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which antibiotic's use for otitis media is limited because up to half of Streptococcus pneumoniae resists its class?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Azithromycin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Azithromycin is a macrolide, and up to 50 percent of Streptococcus pneumoniae resists macrolides, so it is a less reliable penicillin-allergy alternative. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is approved for SUBARACHNOID HEMORRHAGE?","choices":["Nifedipine","Nicardipine","Nimodipine","Isradipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nimodipine's approved indication is subarachnoid hemorrhage. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which drug is the CHOLESTEROL ABSORPTION INHIBITOR, acting at the intestinal brush border?","choices":["Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Evolocumab","Ezetimibe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe selectively inhibits intestinal cholesterol absorption, raising hepatic LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptor expression. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker dilates vessels strongly without changing heart rate or atrioventricular conduction?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amlodipine gives strong vasodilation with heart rate and atrioventricular conduction unchanged. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class binds the 30S subunit to interfere with protein synthesis?","choices":["Azithromycin","Gentamicin","Bacitracin","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycoside mechanism. Its ocular risk is corneal ulceration and reactive keratoconjunctivitis after several days. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class is NOT useful for acute allergy symptoms?","choices":["Ketotifen","Naphazoline","Cromolyn","Epinastine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mast cell stabilizers need 5 to 14 days for full efficacy, so they are useless in the moment. They suit predictable seasonal allergy in patients who cannot tolerate other therapy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is CONTRAINDICATED in children under two years of age?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Dorzolamide","Brimonidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The alpha-2 agonists, because of central nervous system depression and apnea. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which penicillin can be given as a SINGLE INTRAMUSCULAR INJECTION for group A streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["Benzathine penicillin G","Penicillin V potassium","Ampicillin","Dicloxacillin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Benzathine penicillin G is a single intramuscular injection that replaces the full oral course for group A strep pharyngitis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which dihydropyridine is available INTRAVENOUSLY?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nicardipine","Felodipine","Nimodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridines are oral only, except nicardipine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which drug's concentrations are LOWERED BY ANTACIDS?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Alirocumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antacids and bile acid sequestrants decrease ezetimibe concentrations; cyclosporine increases them. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker should be avoided in its short-acting form?","choices":["Amlodipine","Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting agents are to be avoided, and nifedipine is the example named. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, breaking double-stranded DNA?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Erythromycin","Gentamicin","Natamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The fluoroquinolone mechanism: supercoiled DNA cannot relax, and the strand breaks. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which class inhibits mast cell degranulation, limiting release of histamine, tryptase and prostaglandin D2?","choices":["Pheniramine","Nedocromil","Bromfenac","Loteprednol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The mast cell stabilizers, which also dampen mediator release from basophils, eosinophils and neutrophils. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which alpha-2 agonist is MORE LIPOPHILIC?","choices":["Apraclonidine","Timolol","Brimonidine","Pilocarpine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Apraclonidine is highly ionized at physiological pH; brimonidine is the more lipophilic of the two, and allergic conjunctivitis is less common with it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which first-generation cephalosporin is a penicillin-allergy alternative for group A streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["Cefdinir","Ceftriaxone","Cefixime","Cephalexin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin is the narrow first-generation cephalosporin named for penicillin-allergic group A strep pharyngitis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker raises DIGOXIN levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit P-glycoprotein (raising digoxin) and CYP3A4 (raising three statins). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug ACTIVATES PPAR-ALPHA to lower triglycerides and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Fenofibrate","Colestipol","Atorvastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha: more fatty acid oxidation, less VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein), more ApoA-1 and HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which drug class slows heart rate and is the preferred initial therapy when beta blockers are contraindicated or not tolerated?","choices":["Dihydropyridines","Long-acting nitrates","Non-dihydropyridines","Short-acting nitrates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines are the initial therapy when beta blockers cannot be used; dihydropyridines are added to a beta blocker. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic agent antagonizes para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) to block folic acid synthesis?","choices":["Trimethoprim","Polymyxin B","Bacitracin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interfering with folate synthesis stops bacterial growth. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How long should a patient be given to judge the FULL effect of an ophthalmic antihistamine?","choices":["Two weeks","Two days","Six weeks","One day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Onset is within minutes, but full efficacy takes about two weeks to assess. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class reduces aqueous production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body epithelium?","choices":["Bimatoprost","Dorzolamide","Betaxolol","Carbachol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Less bicarbonate, less fluid transport, lower pressure. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is paired with cefixime for sinusitis in a penicillin-allergic patient?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Levofloxacin","Clindamycin","Azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin plus cefixime is one of the penicillin-allergy regimens for sinusitis, and clindamycin is also a pharyngitis alternative. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker RAISES SIMVASTATIN levels by inhibiting CYP3A4?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine","Diltiazem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-dihydropyridines inhibit CYP3A4, raising atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug INCREASES the ANTICOAGULANT EFFECT of warfarin?","choices":["Gemfibrozil","Colesevelam","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which drug class is listed as contraindicated with a heart rate below 60 beats per minute, acute heart failure or an ejection fraction below 40 percent?","choices":["Dihydropyridines","Non-dihydropyridines","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low heart rate, acute heart failure and low ejection fraction are contraindications for the non-dihydropyridines. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which agent binds phospholipids on the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane and lets intracellular contents leak out?","choices":["Trimethoprim","Bacitracin","Polymyxin B","Erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A membrane-permeability agent, partnered with trimethoprim in the combination drop. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic class blocks cyclooxygenase, stopping conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins and thromboxanes?","choices":["Prednisolone","Cyclosporine","Cromolyn","Ketorolac"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ophthalmic non-steroidals. The mechanism is the same whether the drug is taken for a headache or put in the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class causes a BITTER TASTE in about a quarter of patients and stinging in about a third?","choices":["Brinzolamide","Latanoprost","Timolol","Brimonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, which also cause allergic conjunctivitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which ear drop component is the classic cause of ALLERGIC CONTACT SENSITIVITY (hypersensitivity)?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Neomycin","Ofloxacin","Polymyxin B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neomycin, in the Cortisporin drops, carries a chance of hypersensitivity and is the classic cause of contact allergy in ear drops. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which drug is most likely to cause REFLEX TACHYCARDIA?","choices":["Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridines dilate arteries strongly and provoke rebound tachycardia; immediate-release forms are avoided in unstable angina. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which fibrate is named as a RELATIVE CONTRAINDICATION with statins?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Bezafibrate","Ezetimibe","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gemfibrozil sits on the statin relative contraindication list with cyclosporine, niacin and erythromycin, because of myopathy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug is placed under the tongue or sprayed for acute relief of angina?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Isosorbide mononitrate","Amlodipine","Metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitroglycerin, as a sublingual tablet or spray, relieves acute symptoms and prevents effort-induced angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class is PREFERRED for corneal ulcers or suspected Pseudomonas aeruginosa?","choices":["Erythromycin","Levofloxacin","Bacitracin","Sulfacetamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The fluoroquinolones are the reach for ulcers and for the gram-negative rod, though they are expensive and resistance is emerging. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What are the ophthalmic non-steroidals indicated for?","choices":["Natamycin: fungal keratitis","Timolol: glaucoma","Bromfenac: postoperative inflammation","Trifluridine: herpes keratitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ophthalmic non-steroidals such as bromfenac are for postoperative inflammation and pain and for allergic conjunctivitis, and they are NOT routinely recommended for conjunctivitis generally. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which class activates muscarinic receptors, contracting the ciliary muscle to facilitate outflow?","choices":["Timolol","Brimonidine","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cholinergic agonists. Compliance is poor because of side effects and frequent administration. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which ear drop is NOT recommended with a RUPTURED EARDRUM or tubes in place, because of cochlear damage and hearing loss?","choices":["Neomycin/polymyxin B/hydrocortisone","Ciprofloxacin/dexamethasone","Ofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The polymyxin B in these Cortisporin drops can reach the inner ear through a ruptured eardrum or tubes, causing cochlear damage and hearing loss. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective and given INTRAVENOUSLY?","choices":["Atenolol","Esmolol","Bisoprolol","Acebutolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the second-generation, beta-1 selective agent marked for intravenous use. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug is a BILE ACID SEQUESTRANT?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Fenofibrate","Cholestyramine","Pravastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bile acid sequestrants are cholestyramine, colestipol and colesevelam. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which drug must never be combined with nitrates because the result can be severe hypotension, myocardial infarction or stroke?","choices":["Metoprolol","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Sildenafil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5 and must not be used with nitrates. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has conjunctivitis and keratitis has been excluded. Which antibiotic class is preferred, and why?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones, because contact lens wearers are at high risk for Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic class can RAISE intraocular pressure as an adverse effect, alongside lacrimation and keratitis?","choices":["Cromolyn","Nepafenac","Ketotifen","Natamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ophthalmic non-steroidals. Glucocorticoids raise pressure too, but the non-steroidal list is lacrimation, keratitis, raised pressure and irritation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class causes fixed, small pupils, myopia and visual disturbance — and is especially poorly tolerated by YOUNGER patients?","choices":["Latanoprost","Brinzolamide","Carbachol","Betaxolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cholinergic agonists. Younger patients are usually intolerant of miotic therapy because of the visual blurring. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which antifungal is a CYTOCHROME P450 3A4 INHIBITOR that prolongs the corrected QT interval?","choices":["Nystatin","Terbinafine","Caspofungin","Ketoconazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ketoconazole inhibits cytochrome P450 3A4 and prolongs the corrected QT interval (QTc). — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which beta blocker's HIGH LIPID SOLUBILITY brings nightmares and depression?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Nadolol","Sotalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Highly lipid-soluble agents cause more central effects: depression, nightmares, vivid dreams and hallucinations. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED when TRIGLYCERIDES exceed 400 mg/dL?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Colestipol","Gemfibrozil","Niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins may raise VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides; above 400 mg/dL is an absolute contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which drug class blocks phosphodiesterase type 5, the enzyme that breaks down cyclic guanosine monophosphate, making its combination with nitrates dangerous?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Cardioselective beta blockers","Phosphodiesterase inhibitors","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil inhibit phosphodiesterase type 5, which breaks down cyclic guanosine monophosphate. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class carries a risk of corneal ulceration and reactive keratoconjunctivitis after several days of use?","choices":["Erythromycin","Bacitracin","Azithromycin","Tobramycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycoside-specific risk, and one of the few adverse effects worth attaching to a single class. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class suppresses the LATE phase allergic reaction by inhibiting phospholipase A2?","choices":["Prednisolone","Ketorolac","Olopatadine","Lodoxamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The glucocorticoids, cutting off arachidonic acid derived mediators at source. They also inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, reducing scar formation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agonist is used in the SURGICAL setting?","choices":["Pilocarpine","Acetylcholine","Neostigmine","Tropicamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The surgical agent among the three cholinergic agonists. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which antifungal can cause HEPATITIS, cirrhosis and hepatic failure along with high cholesterol and orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Nystatin","Terbinafine","Ketoconazole","Caspofungin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ketoconazole's listed adverse effects include hepatitis, abnormal liver tests, cirrhosis, hepatic failure, hyperlipidemia and orthostatic hypotension. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which beta blocker ALSO BLOCKS ALPHA-1 and is used in heart failure?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atenolol","Nadolol","Carvedilol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade, antioxidant and antiproliferative actions; it is a heart failure agent. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which lipid-lowering drug is APPROVED IN PREGNANCY because it is not absorbed?","choices":["Cholestyramine","Atorvastatin","Fenofibrate","Simvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bile acid sequestrants are not absorbed, so they are approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drug class is avoided in aortic valve stenosis and obstructive cardiomyopathy because of the risk of hypotension?","choices":["Beta blockers","Nitrates","Calcium channel blockers","Fibrinolytics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aortic valve stenosis and obstructive cardiomyopathy are listed nitrate contraindications because of hypotension; phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors are the other. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic class is associated with an unpleasant taste after instillation?","choices":["Bacitracin","Erythromycin","Levofloxacin","Gentamicin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fluoroquinolone effect, and it reflects nasolacrimal drainage carrying drug to the throat. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which glucocorticoid is a SOFT steroid with a lower risk of raising intraocular pressure?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","Difluprednate","Loteprednol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One of the three soft steroids, with fluorometholone and rimexolone. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient's pressure is not controlled on one agent. What is the principle behind adding a second?","choices":["A drug with a DIFFERENT mechanism, for additive effect","Choose an agent of the same class at a higher strength","Add a topical glucocorticoid","Add a topical vasoconstrictor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Targeting a different route lowers pressure additively; fixed combinations can then cut the number of drops. Adding a drug of the same category would be the wrong move. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which antifungal binds to sterols in the fungal cell membrane, raising its permeability?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Nystatin","Terbinafine","Caspofungin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin, a nonabsorbable polyene, binds sterols in the fungal cell membrane and raises its permeability. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which beta blocker blocks alpha-1 AND acts as a BETA-2 AGONIST?","choices":["Propranolol","Bisoprolol","Labetalol","Esmolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Labetalol is a third-generation agent with alpha-1 blockade and beta-2 agonist activity. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which drug INTERFERES WITH ABSORPTION of digoxin, warfarin and thyroxine?","choices":["Evolocumab","Fenofibrate","Rosuvastatin","Colesevelam"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anion-exchange resins bind these drugs in the gut; give them 1 hour before or 4 hours after the resin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug class is worn as a patch or ointment 12 hours on and 12 hours off to limit tolerance?","choices":["Long-acting nitrates","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Antiplatelet drugs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting nitrate patches and ointment follow a 12-hours-on, 12-hours-off schedule to limit tachyphylaxis. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which is the ONLY commercially available ophthalmic antifungal?","choices":["Amphotericin B","Natamycin","Fluconazole","Miconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Everything else has to be compounded by a specialty pharmacy or given systemically. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which ocular glucocorticoid is given by INTRAVITREAL injection?","choices":["Fluorometholone","Rimexolone","Triamcinolone","Loteprednol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The intravitreal agent among the ophthalmic steroids. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the general goal of glaucoma therapy, in terms of pressure?","choices":["A 50 percent reduction","Any reduction at all","Normalizing to below 10 mmHg","A 20 to 30 percent reduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. And therapy can be started in one eye to judge efficacy and tolerability. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which NONABSORBABLE antifungal treats ORAL CANDIDIASIS (thrush) in a patient on inhaled steroids, with human immunodeficiency virus infection or on chemotherapy?","choices":["Nystatin","Ketoconazole","Terbinafine","Caspofungin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin oral suspension is a nonabsorbable antifungal for oral candidiasis, which is seen with inhaled steroids, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection and chemotherapy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is one of the three used for HEART FAILURE?","choices":["Propranolol","Bisoprolol","Atenolol","Timolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart failure beta blockers are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol, started very low and increased slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which drug causes PROSTAGLANDIN-MEDIATED FLUSHING, minimized by aspirin?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Niacin","Colestipol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Niacin flushing is prostaglandin mediated; aspirin premedication minimizes it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which drug class does NOT relieve angina symptoms but may help prevent progression of coronary artery disease?","choices":["Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These inhibitors barely change myocardial oxygen consumption and do not relieve angina, but may slow coronary artery disease. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which antifungal increases fungal cell membrane permeability by binding sterol?","choices":["Natamycin","Fluconazole","Itraconazole","Ketoconazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Natamycin binds sterol in the fungal cell membrane; it covers Aspergillus, Candida, Cephalosporium, Fusarium and Penicillium. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Ocular glucocorticoids are limited to a pulse of what length?","choices":["Less than six weeks","Less than two weeks","Less than 72 hours","No limit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Their risks accumulate: cataract, raised pressure and glaucoma, infection from reduced immune function, delayed wound healing, and corneal ulcers. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which patients with ocular hypertension are treated rather than monitored?","choices":["All patients with raised pressure","Only those already blind in one eye","Those WITH risk factors","Only those under 40"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those without risk factors are monitored until glaucomatous changes occur. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which antifungal causes only stomach upset (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting) because it is NOT ABSORBED?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Terbinafine","Caspofungin","Nystatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin is not absorbed, so its listed side effects are limited to diarrhea, nausea, stomach pain and vomiting. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for GLAUCOMA, reducing aqueous humor production?","choices":["Timolol","Esmolol","Atenolol","Labetalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Timolol reduces aqueous humor production in the ciliary body; systemic effects of the drops are notable. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which drug RAISES URIC ACID and glucose, so gout and diabetes are relative contraindications?","choices":["Gemfibrozil","Niacin","Ezetimibe","Cholestyramine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At larger doses niacin raises liver enzymes, glucose and uric acid; chronic liver disease is its absolute contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which drug is recommended for patients who are allergic to aspirin?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Heparin","Clopidogrel","Alteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clopidogrel is as efficacious as aspirin in secondary prevention and is recommended when aspirin causes allergy. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which topical antiviral interferes with viral replication by inhibiting thymidylate synthetase and taking the place of thymidine in viral DNA?","choices":["Ganciclovir","Acyclovir","Foscarnet","Trifluridine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Used for herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis. Its ocular toxicity is punctate keratopathy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which patients are at greater risk of raised intraocular pressure from ocular steroids?","choices":["Those with a FAMILY HISTORY","Contact lens wearers","Patients on topical antibiotics","Patients with asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated pressure is more likely with a family history of glaucoma, alongside the other steroid risks of cataract, infection and delayed healing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which ocular anesthetic leaves the eye numb for 10 to 20 minutes with NO BLINK REFLEX?","choices":["Tropicamide","Proparacaine","Phenylephrine","Fluorescein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. True of both ocular anesthetics, and the reason you must not write prescriptions for them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which drug is an IRREVERSIBLE, noncompetitive inhibitor of platelets?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Aspirin","Acetaminophen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelets and does not select between cyclooxygenase 1 and 2. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for MIGRAINE PROPHYLAXIS?","choices":["Esmolol","Bisoprolol","Atenolol","Propranolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol (with timolol) is used for migraine prophylaxis at low dose; the mechanism is uncertain. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which form of niacin is NOT EFFECTIVE as an antilipemic?","choices":["Niacinamide","Nicotinic acid","Extended-release niacin","Immediate-release niacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nicotinic acid is the antilipemic form; its amide, niacinamide (nicotinamide), is not effective. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which drug is chewed and swallowed at the first signs of chest pain in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is chewed and swallowed at the first signs of chest pain and reduces mortality and reinfarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which antiviral competitively inhibits the binding of deoxyguanosine triphosphate to DNA polymerase?","choices":["Trifluridine","Foscarnet","Ganciclovir","Cidofovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Used for herpetic keratitis, and for cytomegalovirus retinitis by intravitreal injection. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits production and release of interleukin 2, reducing T cell activation, and is used for chronic dry eye?","choices":["Prednisolone","Ketorolac","Lodoxamide","Cyclosporine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It reduces inflammatory markers in the lacrimal gland and increases tear production. Its commonest adverse effect is ocular burning. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which ocular agents should you NOT write prescriptions for?","choices":["Tetracaine","Latanoprost","Olopatadine","Erythromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ocular anesthetics. Used in clinic for tonometry, foreign body removal and superficial corneal surgery, but not sent home. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which drug is linked to REYE SYNDROME in a child with a viral fever such as chickenpox or influenza?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Naproxen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin in children with fever from a viral illness raises the incidence of Reye syndrome. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is NON-SELECTIVE (first generation)?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atenolol","Nadolol","Bisoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first-generation non-selective agents are nadolol, penbutolol, pindolol, propranolol, sotalol and timolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug produces the LARGEST RISE IN HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Pravastatin","Niacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nicotinic acid raises HDL by 15 to 35%, the largest rise of any class. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug is contraindicated by allergy, a recent gastrointestinal bleed or a recent intracranial hemorrhage?","choices":["Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol","Verapamil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Allergy, recent gastrointestinal bleeding and recent intracranial hemorrhage are the aspirin contraindications. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which antiviral is given INTRAVENOUSLY for cytomegalovirus retinitis?","choices":["Trifluridine","Foscarnet","Famciclovir","Natamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cytomegalovirus retinitis is the intravenous indication, alongside ganciclovir and cidofovir. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Cyclosporine eye drops are indicated for which condition?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Open-angle glaucoma","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca","Herpes simplex keratitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic dry eye associated with inflammation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which class competitively blocks muscarinic receptors to produce mydriasis for a fundoscopic examination?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Pilocarpine","Proparacaine","Tropicamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The antimuscarinic cycloplegics, with atropine and cyclopentolate. Also used in uveitis to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Very low doses of which drug may benefit hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (preeclampsia)?","choices":["Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Acetaminophen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is avoided in pregnancy at ordinary doses, but very low doses may benefit preeclampsia. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which alpha-1 blocker is ALPHA-1A SELECTIVE, treating the prostate with little vascular effect?","choices":["Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Terazosin","Doxazosin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tamsulosin selectively blocks alpha-1A receptors; vascular receptors are alpha-1B, so blood pressure effects are limited. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which drug causes CHOLELITHIASIS and is contraindicated in existing gallbladder disease?","choices":["Niacin","Colestipol","Fenofibrate","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates cause cholelithiasis and are contraindicated in existing gallbladder disease. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which drug used in acute coronary syndrome relieves chest pain but shows no mortality benefit?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Alteplase","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates give only relief of chest pain, with no mortality benefit. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Adenoviral conjunctivitis is diagnosed. Which approach is appropriate?","choices":["Supportive care, no antiviral","Topical trifluridine","Topical ganciclovir","Oral valacyclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adenoviral conjunctivitis runs a self-limited course and is treated with symptomatic relief of irritation; there is no antiviral for it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which systemic conditions manifest with dry eyes?","choices":["Hypertension and hyperlipidemia","Sjogren syndrome and rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis and gout","Peptic ulcer and reflux disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sjogren syndrome and rheumatoid arthritis, with vitamin A deficiency and Stevens-Johnson syndrome, manifest with dry eyes; treat the underlying disease first. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A pupil dilated with which agent stays MORE REACTIVE TO LIGHT?","choices":["Atropine","Cyclopentolate","Phenylephrine","Tropicamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sympathomimetic mydriatic leaves more light reactivity than the antimuscarinics do. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which drug's toxicity causes hyperventilation with alkalosis, then fever, dehydration and metabolic acidosis, then shock and coma?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Acetaminophen","Aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin toxicity (salicylism) runs from hyperventilation and alkalosis to fever and metabolic acidosis, then shock and coma. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which antihypertensive MILDLY LOWERS LDL (low-density lipoprotein) AND TRIGLYCERIDES and raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Prazosin","Propranolol","Clonidine","Hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prazosin shows evidence of a mild fall in LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides with a rise in HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which drug is an injectable MONOCLONAL ANTIBODY against PCSK9?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Alirocumab","Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are the injectable-only, expensive PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug is added for chest pain that does not respond to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Aspirin","Morphine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Morphine, an opioid analgesic, manages chest pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which route of ocular drug delivery circumvents absorption entirely for an immediate local effect?","choices":["Topical","Subconjunctival injection","Intraocular (intracameral) injection","Intravitreal injection or device"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Used for endophthalmitis, retinitis and age-related macular degeneration. Its limitation is retinal toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which principal target cells drive the ocular hypersensitivity reaction?","choices":["Mast cells and basophils","Neutrophils and macrophages","T cells and B cells","Eosinophils and dendritic cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulin E (IgE) binds their Fc receptors, and within seconds tyrosine kinases activate and histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes are released. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which agent reveals epithelial defects of the cornea and conjunctiva?","choices":["Proparacaine","Fluorescein","Tropicamide","Phenylephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Used for anterior segment staining and disclosing corneal injury. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which drug should NOT be taken with anticoagulants, because it inhibits platelet function?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Guaifenesin","Aspirin","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients are told not to take aspirin with anticoagulants because aspirin inhibits platelet function. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which drug is a central ALPHA-2 AGONIST that BLUNTS OPIATE WITHDRAWAL?","choices":["Guanfacine","Prazosin","Hydralazine","Clonidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine's analgesic activity blunts opiate withdrawal reactions; it stimulates central alpha-2 and imidazoline receptors. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which drug's MOST SERIOUS adverse reaction is HYPERSENSITIVITY?","choices":["Evolocumab","Simvastatin","Niacin","Fenofibrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity reactions are the most serious adverse reaction to PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic forms a stable 1:1 complex with plasminogen to convert it to plasmin?","choices":["Alteplase","Streptokinase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Streptokinase forms a stable 1:1 complex with plasminogen, exposing the catalytic site that makes plasmin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which ocular route risks nasal mucosal toxicity and systemic side effects through nasolacrimal absorption?","choices":["Intravitreal injection","Retrobulbar injection","Topical","Intracameral injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Convenient, economical and relatively safe, but what drains down the nasolacrimal duct is absorbed systemically and skips first-pass metabolism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the normal range of intraocular pressure?","choices":["About 30 to 40 mmHg","About 2 to 8 mmHg","About 40 to 60 mmHg","About 10 to 21 mmHg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ocular hypertension is a pressure above normal WITHOUT optic nerve damage or visual field loss. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug raises LITHIUM and methotrexate levels and weakens angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Acetaminophen","Guaifenesin","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen cuts the kidney's clearance of lithium and methotrexate, so their levels rise, and it blunts angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which central agent is the MOST ALPHA-2 SELECTIVE, with LESS SEDATION?","choices":["Clonidine","Guanfacine","Prazosin","Tamsulosin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Guanfacine is less potent than clonidine, the most selective for alpha-2 over alpha-1, less sedating, with occasional withdrawal. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which drug and its active GLUCURONIDE metabolite circulate ENTEROHEPATICALLY?","choices":["Cholestyramine","Atorvastatin","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Enterohepatic recirculation returns ezetimibe to its site of action and limits systemic exposure. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic causes fever, chills and skin rash, making prior exposure a reason to avoid it?","choices":["Alteplase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase","Streptokinase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever, chills and rash occur mainly with streptokinase, and prior exposure or an allergic reaction to it contraindicates reuse. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which prodrug is converted in the eye to epinephrine?","choices":["Dipivefrin","Latanoprost","Brimonidine","Timolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One of the two ocular prodrug examples; the other is latanoprost, converted to prostaglandin F2 alpha. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Glaucoma drugs focus on which type, and why?","choices":["Closed angle, because it is more dangerous","Open angle — that is where drug therapy works","Both equally","Neither — glaucoma is surgical"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angle-closure is an abrupt, emergent blockage of the drainage canal, usually with acute pain, and is not primarily a drug problem. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug causes gastric or duodenal ULCERS, edema, fluid retention and acute renal failure?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Guaifenesin","Ibuprofen","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen's listed adverse effects are gastric or duodenal ulcers, perforation and bleeding, edema and fluid retention, and acute renal failure. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which drug RAISES BLOOD GLUCOSE by inhibiting insulin secretion and retains sodium?","choices":["Lisinopril","Amlodipine","Hydralazine","Clonidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine increases blood glucose by inhibiting insulin secretion and causes sodium retention, so it is often given with a diuretic. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which drug is FIRST LINE when LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering drugs are indicated?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Statins are the most efficacious and best tolerated agents and are first line for LDL lowering. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic is a recombinant protein made in Escherichia coli cells?","choices":["Alteplase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase","Streptokinase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reteplase (Retavase) is produced recombinantly in Escherichia coli cells. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which drug accumulates in the eye and produces a bull's eye lesion?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Chloroquine","Dorzolamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The example given of a drug distributing into and accumulating in the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two routes lower intraocular pressure, and which class does BOTH?","choices":["Latanoprost — it does both","Timolol — it does both","Dorzolamide — it does both","Brimonidine — it increases outflow AND decreases production"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alpha adrenergic agonists appear on both lists. Prostaglandins and cholinergics increase outflow; beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease production. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which over-the-counter pain reliever has a LONGER HALF-LIFE than ibuprofen, so it is taken less often?","choices":["Naproxen","Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Diclofenac"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Naproxen (Aleve) has a longer half-life, so it is dosed less often than ibuprofen. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug is N-ACETYLATED, with LUPUS SYNDROME in slow acetylators?","choices":["Minoxidil","Hydralazine","Nitroprusside","Prazosin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine lupus syndrome risk: high dose, long term, women, slow acetylators and Caucasian ancestry. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic is the tissue plasminogen activator sold as Activase?","choices":["Reteplase","Tenecteplase","Alteplase","Streptokinase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alteplase (Activase) is the recombinant tissue plasminogen activator that directly activates fibrin-bound plasminogen. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Absorption across the cornea is governed by which law?","choices":["Poiseuille's law","Starling's law","Laplace's law","Fick's law"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transcorneal absorption is necessary for local ocular effect, and its rate depends most on the concentration gradient. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which analgesic and antipyretic has NO notable side effects at therapeutic doses?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Naproxen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acetaminophen is very well tolerated, with no notable side effects at therapeutic doses. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which drug should come with the warning that STOOLS MAY TURN BLACK?","choices":["Minoxidil","Hydralazine","Clonidine","Verapamil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stools may turn black on hydralazine; the patient is told ahead of time. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which drug class is contraindicated by aortic dissection and by serious head or facial trauma?","choices":["Nitrates","Beta blockers","Fibrinolytics","Calcium channel blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection and serious head or facial trauma are among the fibrinolytic contraindications. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which drug raises the risk of LIVER DAMAGE (chronically) when combined with alcohol?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Naproxen","Acetaminophen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol with acetaminophen increases the risk of liver damage, especially with chronic use. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which drug OPENS ATP-MODULATED POTASSIUM CHANNELS and causes HYPERTRICHOSIS?","choices":["Minoxidil","Hydralazine","Nitroprusside","Diltiazem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Minoxidil hyperpolarizes arteriolar smooth muscle through potassium efflux and grows hair on the face, back, arms and legs. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which drug is NOT recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome because bleeding risk outweighs benefit?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Alteplase","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolytics such as alteplase are not recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which drug is traditionally used for ACETAMINOPHEN TOXICITY and, inhaled, splits the disulfide bonds in mucus?","choices":["Guaifenesin","Dornase alfa","N-acetylcysteine","Hypertonic saline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen toxicity and, nebulized, splits disulfide bonds that link mucoproteins. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which drug given by INFUSION for HYPERTENSIVE CRISIS can cause CYANIDE toxicity?","choices":["Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Nicardipine","Nitroprusside"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitroprusside carries five cyanide groups; sodium thiosulfate limits cyanide toxicity, and thiocyanate builds up with long infusions or renal failure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which platelet-directed drug class is used before percutaneous coronary intervention and as clot prophylaxis with stent placement?","choices":["P2Y12 receptor antagonists","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors","Heparins","Fibrinolytics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. P2Y12 receptor antagonists are used before percutaneous coronary intervention and as clot prophylaxis with a stent. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which inhaled mucus-thinning drug leaves a ROTTEN EGG smell because of its high sulfur content?","choices":["Guaifenesin","N-acetylcysteine","Dornase alfa","Hypertonic saline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled N-acetylcysteine smells of rotten eggs because of its sulfur content, and it can cause nausea, vomiting and bronchospasm. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED in CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE and the ELDERLY because of reflex sympathetic workload?","choices":["Metoprolol","Lisinopril","Hydralazine","Amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine is contraindicated in coronary artery disease, the elderly and ischemia: vasodilation plus sympathetic activation raises cardiac work. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker is the alternative to a beta blocker when left ventricular function is decreased?","choices":["Verapamil","Diltiazem","Nifedipine","Amlodipine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With decreased left ventricular function, a beta blocker is first line and amlodipine is the calcium channel blocker alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which nebulized drug is a DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) enzyme that thins cystic fibrosis sputum?","choices":["Dornase alfa","N-acetylcysteine","Guaifenesin","Hypertonic saline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dornase alfa cleaves DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) released from degenerating neutrophils, which lowers the viscosity of cystic fibrosis sputum. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug class is first line for angina in a patient with bradycardia or atrioventricular block?","choices":["Non-dihydropyridines","Dihydropyridines","Beta blockers","Antiplatelet drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With bradycardia or atrioventricular block, dihydropyridines are first line and long-acting nitrates the alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which inhaled solution improves AIRWAY HYDRATION and mucociliary clearance?","choices":["Dornase alfa","N-acetylcysteine","Hypertonic saline","Guaifenesin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled hypertonic saline improves mucus flow properties, airway hydration, mucociliary clearance and lung function. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which antianginal's patient education includes a warning about constipation?","choices":["Metoprolol","Nitroglycerin","Isosorbide mononitrate","Verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Education for calcium channel blockers such as verapamil includes dizziness and constipation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which first-generation antihistamine is sold over the counter as a SLEEP AID?","choices":["Doxylamine","Cetirizine","Fexofenadine","Loratadine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Doxylamine is the first-generation antihistamine sold over the counter as a sleep aid, because sedation is the major side effect. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which drug class should be avoided in variant (Prinzmetal) angina because it may worsen symptoms?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers","Nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In variant angina, calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce symptoms, but beta blockers may worsen them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which first-generation antihistamine is rated HIGH for sedative, antiemetic and anticholinergic effects alike?","choices":["Chlorpheniramine","Meclizine","Promethazine","Loratadine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Promethazine is the first-generation antihistamine rated high for sedative, antiemetic and anticholinergic effects, with the strongest antimuscarinic actions against motion sickness. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which ORAL second-generation antihistamine is rated LOW, rather than very low, for sedation?","choices":["Fexofenadine","Loratadine","Chlorpheniramine","Cetirizine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cetirizine is rated low for sedation, while the other oral second-generation agents, fexofenadine and loratadine, are rated very low. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which intranasal antihistamine causes a BITTER TASTE and nosebleeds?","choices":["Azelastine","Cetirizine","Fexofenadine","Loratadine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Azelastine (Astelin) is the intranasal histamine-1 (H1) antagonist whose side effects are a bitter taste and epistaxis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which histamine receptor type increases postcapillary permeability, mucus secretion and bronchoconstriction?","choices":["Histamine-2 (H2) receptors","Histamine-1 (H1) receptors","Histamine-3 (H3) receptors","Histamine-4 (H4) receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histamine-1 (H1) receptors raise postcapillary permeability and mucus secretion and cause bronchoconstriction. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which histamine receptor type increases GASTRIC ACID secretion and the force of heart contraction?","choices":["Histamine-1 (H1) receptors","Histamine-3 (H3) receptors","Histamine-2 (H2) receptors","Histamine-4 (H4) receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Histamine-2 (H2) receptors raise gastric acid and pepsin secretion and the force of heart contraction. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which class of drugs causes MAJOR SEDATION that is additive with alcohol?","choices":["Second-generation antihistamines","Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants","First-generation antihistamines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. First-generation histamine-1 (H1) blockers cross into the central nervous system, so sedation is the major side effect. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which class of drugs HARDLY enters the central nervous system, so it causes much LESS SEDATION?","choices":["Second-generation antihistamines","First-generation antihistamines","Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Second-generation histamine-1 (H1) blockers largely stay out of the central nervous system, so they cause much less sedation. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which nasal corticosteroid is sold as FLONASE?","choices":["Mometasone","Fluticasone","Triamcinolone","Beclomethasone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fluticasone is sold as Flonase; Flovent is the inhaled asthma version, so the names must not be mixed up. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which nasal corticosteroid is sold as NASONEX?","choices":["Flunisolide","Budesonide","Mometasone","Triamcinolone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mometasone is the nasal corticosteroid sold as Nasonex. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which nasal corticosteroid is sold as BECONASE AQ?","choices":["Fluticasone","Budesonide","Flunisolide","Beclomethasone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beclomethasone is the nasal corticosteroid sold as Beconase AQ. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which class of drugs causes NOSEBLEEDS, septal perforation and an unpleasant taste?","choices":["Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants","Expectorants","Antitussives"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nasal corticosteroids mostly cause local effects: epistaxis, septal perforation and an unpleasant taste. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Prednisone is converted in the liver to which active glucocorticoid, sold as the oral liquid Orapred?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","Methylprednisolone","Hydrocortisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prednisolone (Orapred) is the active form of prednisone and is the liquid formulation, while prednisone (Deltasone) is the tablet. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which class causes hypokalemia, TENDON RUPTURE, cataracts and glaucoma?","choices":["Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants","Systemic corticosteroids","Expectorants"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic steroids such as prednisone and dexamethasone cause low potassium, tendon rupture, cataracts, glaucoma and weakened immunity. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which class of drugs carries the counseling to avoid LIVE VACCINES and exposure to chickenpox and measles?","choices":["Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants","Antitussives","Systemic corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A patient on a systemic steroid such as prednisone is immunocompromised at immunosuppressive doses, so live vaccines and exposure to chickenpox and measles are avoided. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which nasal spray causes REBOUND RHINITIS (rhinitis medicamentosa) if used for more than a few days?","choices":["Oxymetazoline","Azelastine","Mometasone","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxymetazoline (Afrin) causes rebound rhinitis (rhinitis medicamentosa) if used for more than 3 to 5 days. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which nasal spray interacts with MONOAMINE OXIDASE INHIBITORS and antidepressants?","choices":["Azelastine","Oxymetazoline","Mometasone","Fluticasone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oxymetazoline's listed interactions are with monoamine oxidase inhibitors and antidepressants. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which drug can cause TACHYCARDIA, hypertension and headache, and weaken the effect of blood pressure medicines?","choices":["Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","Pseudoephedrine","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pseudoephedrine (Sudafed) is an oral sympathomimetic decongestant; it can cause tachycardia and hypertension and decreases the effect of antihypertensives. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which antitussive ANESTHETIZES the stretch receptors in the lungs?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Guaifenesin","N-acetylcysteine","Benzonatate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate (Tessalon) anesthetizes the stretch receptors in the lungs to relieve a non-productive cough. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which cough drug is contraindicated with an allergy to TETRACAINE or related products?","choices":["Benzonatate","Dextromethorphan","Guaifenesin","N-acetylcysteine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is contraindicated in allergy to the product or related products such as tetracaine. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which antitussive numbs the mouth if its capsule is CHEWED?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Benzonatate","Codeine","Guaifenesin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is swallowed whole; chewing the capsule causes local anesthesia of the mouth. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which antitussive is related to codeine and suppresses the cough center in the medulla through sigma receptor activation?","choices":["Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","Dextromethorphan","Dornase alfa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dextromethorphan (Robitussin, Delsym) is related to codeine and suppresses the medullary cough center through sigma receptors. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which cough drug is contraindicated within 2 weeks of taking a MONOAMINE OXIDASE INHIBITOR?","choices":["Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","N-acetylcysteine","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dextromethorphan raises the risk of serotonin syndrome, so it is contraindicated during or within 2 weeks of a monoamine oxidase inhibitor. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which drug's counseling is to drink plenty of fluid and expect INCREASED DRAINAGE?","choices":["Guaifenesin","Dextromethorphan","Benzonatate","Dornase alfa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Guaifenesin loosens mucus and decreases its viscosity, so patients drink plenty of fluid and expect more drainage. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
